\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

All experiments use Qwen3-32B and the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}) unless noted; cross-model validation covers Dimensions~1--2, while secondary analyses (error taxonomy, DocRED $R^2$, TOST) remain single-model.

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

\looseness=-1
Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a shows RE accuracy declines monotonically with schema size (0.86$\to$0.50 for FewRel). NER recall declines through intermediate counts (0.51$\to$0.27, plateauing across counts 22--44) before partial recovery at full-schema (0.41), where eliminating type-filtering overhead restores extraction performance. DocRED replicates the monotonic RE pattern (88.0\%$\to$38.7\%, $-$56\%; Appendix). MAVEN exhibits a reversed trend attributable to centrality confounding (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). The effect replicates on Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($-$43\,pp on Few-NERD; per-batch $r = 0.725$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}). The pattern is stable across three seeds (max $\Delta = 10.7$\,pp, within 1\,SD; intra-batch variance exceeds inter-seed variance by ${>}$5$\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:variance-decomposition}). Formal trend analysis confirms monotonic RE decline (Jonckheere-Terpstra $J = 109{,}707$, $p < 0.001$). Count decline replicates on LLaMA-70B with comparable magnitude and on Gemini Flash (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}). Count curves establish trend direction; magnitude estimation relies on the higher-powered BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate (the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$) and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & ${<}\,0.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. Confusion rate is bidirectional $C(t_i{\to}t_j) + C(t_j{\to}t_i)$ divided by gold frequency sum; CIs are percentile bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$). FewRel ($r = 0.187$; \S\ref{sec:scs-validation}) excluded (per-instance RE format). Type-level cluster bootstrap CIs exclude zero (Few-NERD [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN [0.081, 0.185]).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1
The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$. Type-level cluster bootstrap yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$). Spearman correlations corroborate: $\rho = 0.144\text{--}0.227$ across all pairs, strengthening to $\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$ among pairs with nonzero confusion (though conditioning on the dependent variable may inflate this).

\looseness=-1
The remaining variance reflects entity frequency, prompt position \citep{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention ambiguity, and definition quality. While pairwise $r^2$ is modest, correlated type pairs within batches compound this signal into substantial batch-level RE accuracy variance (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}). The independent MiniLM embedding yields a \emph{stronger} SCS--confusion correlation on Few-NERD ($r = 0.172$ vs.\ Qwen3 $r = 0.138$; Appendix), indicating that shared-representation bias is conservative. SCS outperforms simpler baselines (Levenshtein, WordNet Wu-Palmer, same-parent; Appendix~\ref{app:scs-baselines}). Appendix~\ref{app:error-analysis} lists the most confusable type pairs with concrete examples.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_error_taxonomy.pdf}
\caption{Error taxonomy decomposition (Few-NERD NER and FewRel RE). IntraSub scales monotonically with BCS across all evaluated datasets ($\rho = 0.84\text{--}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} includes DocRED). RE missed rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER missed rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic.}
\label{fig:error_taxonomy}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

\looseness=-1
On FewRel, BCS independently explains ${\sim}$27\% of cross-batch RE accuracy variance (semipartial $R^2 = 0.273$, $n = 204$, after controlling for degree centrality)---the primary quantitative evidence for confusability as a predictor alongside count. We fix type count and vary BCS to isolate this effect. FewRel's balanced design (700 instances per type) eliminates type frequency as a confound;\footnote{Partial correlation controlling for degree centrality: FewRel $r_{\text{partial}} = -0.537$ ($p < 0.001$, VIF${}\,=\,1.18$). DocRED partial correlation ($r_{\text{partial}} = -0.348$, $p < 0.001$) is reported for completeness, but VIF${}\,=\,50.9$ (BCS--centrality $r = 0.99$) renders the estimate unstable; the clean evidence for BCS's independent contribution comes from FewRel (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_partial_corr}, Table~\ref{tab:partial_corr}).} the within-pool analysis (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}) controls for pool composition.

In BCS-controlled FewRel subsets, the high-BCS quartile shows $\Delta=-5.3$\,pp lower accuracy than low-BCS (quartile comparison on 100 random batches; based on a single extreme batch per BCS level at 16-rel scale; full data in Appendix~\ref{app:bcs-controlled-table}). At 80 relations, extreme-batch construction amplifies this to $-$31.2\,pp (95\% CI [17.7, 42.7]), stable across count levels. An intermediate count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation (40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool) yields consistent gaps ($-$33.3\,pp at count 8, $-$31.8\,pp at count 16) and confirms monotonic decline.

\paragraph{Document-level RE (DocRED).}
The effect replicates on DocRED's 96-relation schema ($n{=}55$ per count level;\footnote{DocRED uses 5 batches per count level (vs.\ 25 for FewRel) due to higher per-batch evaluation cost; CIs are correspondingly wider.}): extreme-BCS gap $-$25.0\,pp at count 16 ($\rho = -0.762$, 95\% CI [$-$0.88, $-$0.58]) and $-$15.7\,pp at count 32 ($\rho = -0.693$, 95\% CI [$-$0.83, $-$0.50]; both $p < 0.001$). Pooled across counts, $R^2 = 0.578$ ($n = 110$; 95\% CI [0.46, 0.68]; per-count: $R^2 = 0.653$ at count 16, $0.614$ at count 32). The substantially higher $R^2$ relative to FewRel's pooled semipartial $R^2 = 0.273$ largely reflects BCS--centrality collinearity (VIF${}\,=\,50.9$): semipartial $R^2_{\text{BCS}} = 0.056$ after partialing degree centrality, confirming that 90\% of the explained variance is shared with centrality. Given VIF${}\,=\,50.9$, the partial correlation standard errors are inflated approximately 7-fold; this result serves as directional evidence that the BCS--accuracy relationship extends beyond FewRel's schema, not as independent quantitative support. Ridge regression confirms the negative direction under regularization (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_partial_corr}). The clean evidence for BCS's independent contribution comes from FewRel (semipartial $R^2 = 0.273$, VIF${}\,=\,1.18$).

\paragraph{Within-pool controlled comparison.}
\label{sec:within_pool}
Within-pool analysis confirms slope consistency across pools (permutation $p \approx 0.60$; Appendix~\ref{app:within-pool-method}). The 80-relation extreme-batch gap (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) reflects wider BCS range, not stronger per-unit sensitivity.

\paragraph{Practical magnitude.}
\label{sec:practical_magnitude}
\looseness=-1
Direct regression on 100 random 16-type batches from the 80-relation pool yields a conservative restricted-range estimate: $r = -0.22$, $R^2 \approx 0.05$, $p = 0.027$, with BCS explaining ${\sim}$5\% of cross-batch accuracy variance (80-pool BCS SD${}\,=\,0.044$ vs.\ pooled SD${}\,=\,0.141$). The pooled analysis across count levels and pool sizes (semipartial $R^2 = 0.273$, $n = 204$; \S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) provides the primary variance estimate. DocRED pooled $R^2 = 0.578$ ($n = 110$; semipartial $R^2_{\text{BCS}} = 0.056$ after centrality; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_partial_corr}). The typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI [0.4, 4.0]). Quartile comparison confirms: bottom-quartile BCS batches average 82.8\% vs.\ 78.9\% for top-quartile ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp). The amplification from pairwise $r^2 \approx 1.5\%$ to pooled semipartial $R^2 \approx 27.3\%$ is not a statistical artifact but the operative mechanism. Correlated confusable-type interactions compound within shared prompts \citep{robinson-1950-ecological,baan-2023-ecological}, producing the batch-level signal that makes BCS practically predictive (${\sim}3\times$ amplification illustrated by Monte Carlo simulation; Appendix~\ref{app:aggregation-sim}).

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
\looseness=-1
The effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: extreme-BCS gaps of $-$27.1\,pp (count 8) and $-$23.9\,pp (count 16), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's gap (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_bcs}). All three open-weight families (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-70B, LLaMA-8B) show consistent negative BCS slopes on FewRel RE. Gemini 2.5 Flash provides partial but fragile corroboration ($\Delta = -17.6$\,pp at count 8) with substantial leverage sensitivity (leave-one-out Pearson: $r = -0.80 \to -0.42$, $p = 0.22$; Spearman: $\rho = -0.40 \to -0.18$, $p = 0.64$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_model_gemini}). LLaMA-8B and LLaMA-70B both show no NER BCS signal ($r{=}{-}0.13$, $p{=}0.36$ for 70B), confirming the task asymmetry across three models (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_ner_null}).

\paragraph{Prompt sensitivity.}
\looseness=-1
Under the v2 prompt, the extreme-batch BCS gap is $-$25.5\,pp ($r = -0.919$, $p < 0.001$; $n{=}25$), modestly attenuated from v1 but highly significant. NER shows no batch-level BCS--F1 correlation under either prompt ($r \approx 0$, $n = 450$; \S\ref{sec:multi_channel}). The effect holds across four structurally distinct prompt variants at count 32 (all $r \leq -0.47$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompt_variants}), ruling out prompt design as the source. Few-shot demonstrations widen the confusability gap (+4.3\,pp under 5-shot), though this finding is limited to a single dataset and count level (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}).

\subsection{Multi-Dimensional Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

\looseness=-1
Error taxonomy decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy} and Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy}) shows intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS across all three datasets: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$) for Few-NERD NER, $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 0.001$) for FewRel sentence-level RE, and $\rho = 0.843$ ($p < 10^{-30}$, $n = 110$) for DocRED document-level RE. The slightly lower DocRED correlation reflects additional noise from document-level context and cross-sentence reasoning. In NER, missed rates are higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic; confusable types impair entity detection itself. In DocRED, IntraSub accounts for 72\% of errors in high-BCS batches vs.\ 15\% in low-BCS batches; ExtraSub is zero (forced-choice format). Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches. Cross-model replication confirms the IntraSub--BCS correlation on FewRel RE: Gemini~2.5~Flash ($\rho = 0.821$, $p < 0.001$, $n = 60$) and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($\rho = 0.430$, $p = 0.001$, $n = 54$; Table~\ref{tab:cross_model_error_taxonomy}).

\looseness=-1
These results establish two dimensions: \textbf{Dimension~1} (count predicts recall decline) and \textbf{Dimension~2} (confusability predicts type confusion). The full-range regression ($n = 69$, five count levels) serves as the primary separability test; the $2 \times 2$ factorial ($n = 54$) as a sensitivity check. We select the equivalence margin via convergent evidence: (i)~an interaction exceeding the main effect magnitude would undermine dimensional separability; (ii)~Cohen's $d = 0.5$ \citep{cohen-1988-statistical} (medium effect) at the within-cell SD of 4.6\,pp yields $\pm 2.3$\,pp. Both converge on $\pm 2\text{--}3$\,pp.\footnote{Margin sensitivity (Table~\ref{tab:tost_sensitivity}) confirms equivalence from $\pm 2$\,pp ($p = 0.047$) through $\pm 5$\,pp.} On the linear scale, TOST \citep{lakens-2017-equivalence,schuirmann-1987-tost} bounds the FewRel interaction within $\pm 3$\,pp ($p = 0.003$); at $\pm 2$\,pp, equivalence is marginal ($p = 0.047$). Probability-scale TOST is the primary analysis because accuracy values (0.37--0.86) remain in the mid-range where logit and linear scales yield similar inference. The logit-scale result is inconclusive ($p = 0.209$; regression-based TOST: $p = 0.050$ at $\pm 3$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). On DocRED, the raw factorial interaction ($-$9.4\,pp) is confounded by narrower BCS ranges at count 32; range-matched analysis reduces it to 0.93\,pp, consistent with bounded interaction (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_factorial}). In NER, batch-level BCS signal is not detected at current power (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}). Two factors explain the asymmetry: (1)~NER errors distribute across miss, boundary, and type modes rather than concentrating in type substitution (relaxed matching confirms this is not a metric artifact; Appendix~\ref{app:relaxed-matching}), and (2)~BCS range restriction (SD ratio 19\%) limits statistical detection power to 65\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ner_power}). The factorial test is inconclusive ($p = 0.50$). At $n{=}450$ with BCS SD${}\,=\,0.027$, the minimum detectable correlation is $|r| \approx 0.132$ ($\alpha = 0.05$, power${}\,=\,0.80$), while FewRel's $r = -0.57$ would attenuate to $r \approx -0.11$ under this SD compression. Current evidence cannot distinguish absence of a BCS$\to$NER effect from an effect too small to detect---absence of evidence rather than evidence of absence. Post-hoc mitigation attempts---type renaming to reduce SCS and few-shot prompting---fail to close the confusability gap (Appendices~\ref{app:renaming},~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}), confirming that BCS captures a structural property of the schema--model interaction rather than a surface-level naming artifact.

\subsection{Centrality as a Potential Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}
\looseness=-1
Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--F1 correlation ($\rho = +0.184\text{--}0.208$, $p < 0.03$): FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly \citep{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}, conflating confusability with centrality. Controlling for log frequency and degree centrality, the correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}), though the pairwise SCS signal remains intact ($r = 0.129$, $p < 0.001$). Few-NERD's cross-domain schema shows the expected negative direction ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$); mean BCS is reliable only for cross-domain schemas.

